content.theory:
Java is a **statically typed**, compiled language — every variable has a type known before the program runs. It powers Android, enterprise systems, and millions of backend servers.

Every Java program starts in a `main` method. Here's the classic first program:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello, world!");
    }
}
```

Breaking it down:

- `public class Main` — every file has a class; the name must match the file name
- `public static void main(String[] args)` — the entry point Java looks for
- `System.out.println(...)` — prints a line to the console
- **Every statement ends with a semicolon `;`**
- `{ }` define blocks — the opening/closing braces of class and method

`println` adds a newline; `print` does not:

```java
System.out.print("Hi ");
System.out.print("there");
System.out.println("!");   // Hi there!
```

